    "title": "How Does Purdue University--Main Campus Rank Among America's Best Colleges?",
    "text": "Purdue University—Main Campus is a public institution that was founded in 1869. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Purdue University—Main Campus is ranked No. #46 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #11 in Most Innovative Schools. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 44,819 (fall 2024), and the campus size is 2,468 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Purdue University—Main Campus is 15:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Purdue University—Main Campus accepts the Common Application and has a test-optional admissions policy. The school's in-state tuition and fees are $9,992; out-of-state tuition and fees are $28,794. Thirty percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $14,000. The four-year graduation rate is 65%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $60,838.Purdue University—Main Campus is a public institution that was founded in 1869. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Purdue University—Main Campus is ranked No. #46 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #11 in Most Innovative Schools. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 44,819 (fall 2024), and the campus size is 2,468 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Purdue University—Main Campus is 15:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Purdue University—Main Campus accepts the Common Application and has a test-optional admissions policy. The school's in-state tuition and fees are $9,992; out-of-state tuition and fees are $28,794. Thirty percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $14,000. The four-year graduation rate is 65%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $60,838.",
